  "query_preview": "(Today through Saturday)...\nIssued at 248 AM EDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nEarly This Morning...\n\nSome upper energy along with the old surface front were generating \nsome scattered showers across the northeastern half of the forecast \narea. \n\nAs an associated surface low across western Illinois moves east and \nthe initial upper energy departs, the scattered showers should push \neast and north. Most of central Indiana should be dry by 12Z.\n\nToday and Tonight...\n\nAnother upper wave will move east and provide forcing this \nafternoon. Meanwhile, the surface low will move southeast across \nthe area. \n\nWith little or weak forcing across the area this morning, will only \nhave some low PoPs across the far north where the front will start \nout.\n\nDuring the afternoon, as the surface low moves through, forcing will \nincrease. The low will drag the front south. Thus, expect scattered \nto numerous showers and thunderstorms to develop.\n\nInterestingly, some drier air will get advected into the southern \nforecast area ahead of the surface low. This may limit convection \ndevelopment there until the surface front gets closer toward 00Z.\n\nCAMs show a variety of solutions, while other guidance focuses rain \nnorth, near and behind the front. Given all the above, will go \nlikely PoPs in the far north mid to late afternoon, tapering to \nslight chance in the far south. Confidence isn't great as mesoscale \ninfluences as well as the aforementioned dry air could change things.\n\nIf enough instability builds, cannot rule out an isolated severe \nstorm with damaging winds ahead of the front. Locally heavy rain \nremains a threat with precipitable water values near 2 inches.\n\nRain chances will continue tonight as the front continues to trek \nsouth and additional upper energy moves through. Overall though \nforcing doesn't look great, so will keep PoPs no higher than chance \ncategory.\n\nA strong temperature gradient will exist again today as the front \nmoves south. Trimmed blended guidance far north given the expected \nlocation of the front and clouds/rain chances. Went near 80 far \nnorth to the lower 90s south.\n\nMonday and Tuesday...\n\nUpper waves on Monday followed by an approaching upper low on \nTuesday will keep chances for rain around. The hot air will finally \nbe south of the area. Highs will be in the upper 60s to near 80 on \nMonday with only mid 60s to lower 70s Tuesday.\n\nWednesday and beyond...\n\nThe far east may see an isolated shower Wednesday as the upper low \nexits. Otherwise, high pressure will provide dry weather into next \nweekend. Temperatures will start out below normal, but they will \nreturn to closer to normal next weekend.",
